{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a prominent figure in the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Levant. He was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at a young age after his father's assassination in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly a decade until his release in 1174. During his captivity *Regency in Antioch* Raymond's cousin Bohemond III of Antioch was captured by the same forces and a regency was established in Antioch with Raymond III as regent once he was released. As regent Raymond III worked to stabilize the situation in Antioch and managed to secure a peace treaty with the Muslim forces.\n\nUpon his return to Tripoli Raymond III found that the county was in disarray and he worked to reassert his authority. He was also involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the succession crisis that followed the death of King Amalric I in 1174. *Succession Crisis in Jerusalem* Raymond III supported the claim of Miles of Plancy to the throne but ultimately backed the successful candidacy of Baldwin IV. Raymond III was a key advisor to Baldwin IV and played a significant role in the early years of his reign.\n\nRaymond III was also known for his military campaigns against the Muslim forces in the region. He worked to strengthen the defenses of the County of Tripoli and launched several successful attacks against the enemy. Despite his military successes Raymond III was ultimately unable to prevent the loss of the County of Tripoli to Saladin in 1187. Raymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV C